# by F.C.
#
# Use flags for custom hardware (see also /etc/portage/make.conf/00-devices.conf).


# --------- Settings shared with my laptop (Lenovo Legion Pro 5 16ADR10) ---------

# Use HIP only for AMD video cards.
dev-util/hip                    -video_cards_nvidia

# Install the NVIDIA dynamic boost daemon. This is required to boost the GPU
# power on some laptops, based on power profile and usage (without this daemon
# the GPU will be capped to the minimum power used for the power-save profile).
x11-drivers/nvidia-drivers      powerd


# ================================================================================
#  AI BACKEND SETTINGS FOR MY DESKTOP (CPU Ryzen 5900X + GPU Radeon RX 6900 XT)
# ================================================================================

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Use Vulkan for LLMs (max stability and agility, faster than ROCm on my GPU)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   LLM weights are linear. The engine chunks layers into hundreds of sequential,
#   low-footprint VRAM buffers. It rarely allocates massive single blocks, so
#   Vulkan offers advantages: exceptional responsiveness, faster prompt ingestion,
#   and zero-delay initialization via Mesa. Highly mature. Possible Exception:
#   extreme KV Caches (huge contexts like 64k+ tokens on heavy models) can rarely
#   exceed Vulkan allocation limits. Fix with `-ctk q8_0` or `-ctk q4_0`.

# LLAMA.CPP
sci-misc/llama-cpp              -rocm vulkan

# OLLAMA
sci-ml/ggml                     -rocm vulkan


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Use ROCm for IMAGE GENERATION / GPU COMPUTE (avoids buffer crashes)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Diffusion models require massive geometric computations. During the final VAE
#   decoding stage at 1024x1024, the engine demands a monolithic, single contiguous
#   compute buffer of roughly ~6.6 to 8 GB. The AMD RDNA2 architecture (Navi 21) has
#   a hard hardware limit under Vulkan (`maxBufferSize`) restricted to exactly 4 GB.
#   Vulkan cannot allocate the huge VAE block in one go, triggering a fatal `Requested
#   buffer size exceeds device buffer size limit` (OutOfDeviceMemory) crash. ROCm maps
#   VRAM virtually and continuously (HIP/AMD GPU architecture), bypassing Vulkan
#   geometric constraints. It comfortably runs the whole image pipeline in VRAM
#   at native resolutions. Text processing should be offloaded to save some VRAM.

# PYTORCH & C.
dev-python/cupy                 rocm
sci-misc/stable-diffusion-cpp   rocm -vulkan
sci-ml/caffe2                   rocm          # Pytorch < 2.13 only.
sci-ml/pytorch                  rocm          # Pytorch 2.13 and above.
sci-ml/torchvision              rocm
